  "answer_to_user": "These are large, established US companies that pay a solid, sustainable dividend. A dividend is a regular cash payment the company sends to shareholders out of its profits. I screened for a meaningful (not sky-high) yield of 3-8%, a 10-year track record of never cutting the payout, and a payout ratio under ~75% so the dividend isn't stretched beyond what the company earns. Names that came up include PepsiCo (PEP, ~4.1%), Kimberly-Clark (KMB, ~4.7%), Verizon (VZ, ~5.7%), General Mills (GIS, ~6.1%), Pfizer (PFE, ~6.1%), Prudential (PRU, ~4.6%), American Tower (AMT, ~4.0%) and Exelon (EXC, ~3.8%) — all with a 10-year no-cut streak. I deliberately capped the yield to avoid \"yield traps\" (unusually high yields that often signal trouble). Important: no stock is truly risk-free — 'safe' is relative, dividends can still be cut, and prices move. This is for research, not a buy recommendation."
}